//? Title: Choose a cluster count using the gap statistic
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare observed dispersion with a uniform reference

K-means can reduce within-cluster dispersion by adding clusters. The gap
statistic compares this reduction with the one obtained from data drawn
uniformly from an observed bounding box. This example aligns the box
with the principal axes and uses squared Euclidean dispersion.

The 48 synthetic observations form two separated groups. For each
candidate count, the gap is the mean simulated log dispersion minus
the observed log dispersion. The selection rule takes the first
count whose gap is at least the next gap minus the next simulation
error.

```{cpp, id=gap-statistic-reference-box, file="figures/gap_statistic_curve.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/gap_statistic.hpp>

#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    std::vector<double> values;
    for (const int group : {-1, 1})
        for (const double v : {-.3, -.1, .1, .3})
            for (const double u : {-.5, -.3, -.1, .1, .3, .5}) {
                values.push_back(4 * group + u);
                values.push_back(group + .45 * u + v);
            }
    const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> x(48, 2, std::move(values));
    const auto fit = dm::stats::gap_statistic(x,
        {.max_clusters = 4, .reference_samples = 80, .seed = 114}, {.n_init = 9, .seed = 27});

    std::println("The rule selects {} clusters.\n", fit.selected_clusters);
    std::println("| Clusters | Observed log dispersion | Reference mean log dispersion | Gap | Simulation error |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> counts, gap, lower, upper;
    for (const auto& row : fit.entries) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
            row.clusters, row.log_dispersion, row.reference_mean_log_dispersion,
            row.gap, row.simulation_error);
        counts.push_back(row.clusters);
        gap.push_back(row.gap);
        lower.push_back(row.gap - row.simulation_error);
        upper.push_back(row.gap + row.simulation_error);
    }
    const auto chosen = fit.selected_clusters - 1;
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(counts, gap, "", {29, 95, 154}, 2.6)
        .error_bars(counts, lower, upper, .12, {29, 95, 154}, 1.6)
        .points(counts, gap, "", {29, 95, 154}, 4)
        .points({counts[chosen]}, {gap[chosen]}, "Selected count", {176, 74, 44}, 6)
        .title("Gap statistic across candidate counts")
        .x_label("Number of clusters").y_label("Gap")
        .x_ticks({1, 2, 3, 4}, {"1", "2", "3", "4"})
        .x_limits(.75, 4.25).y_limits(-1.22, 2.88).size(740, 420);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/gap_statistic_curve.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The rule selects 2 clusters.

| Clusters | Observed log dispersion | Reference mean log dispersion | Gap | Simulation error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 6.716 | 5.874 | -0.842 | 0.132 |
| 2 | 2.212 | 4.482 | 2.270 | 0.132 |
| 3 | 1.864 | 3.620 | 1.756 | 0.139 |
| 4 | 1.327 | 3.034 | 1.708 | 0.136 |
\image(figures/gap_statistic_curve.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Bars extend one simulation error above and below each gap. The orange point marks the selected count.)
\alttext(The gap rises from below zero at one cluster to a peak at two. The gaps for three and four clusters are lower. Two clusters are selected.)

The sharp improvement from one to two clusters is much larger than
the improvement seen in the uniform samples. The subsequent counts
give smaller gaps, so the rule stops at two.

[Tibshirani, Walther, and Hastie (2001)|https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9868.00293]
